# Hermes Mobile

## Overview

**Creative North Star: "A paper workspace for conversation"**

The conversation reads down one margin. A small speaker name introduces each turn, and a pending question stands out against the paper as a bordered surface. The person can answer where the question appears and still see the message composer below.

The same paper, ink and teal carry through to the atelier. A person can ask Hermes for a review form, try it in the conversation that produced it and pin it there for later. Its fields prepare an editable message. Technical views make their units, scope and available actions legible in French.

**Key Characteristics:**

- One reading column for speakers, messages and decisions.
- A quiet teal action color shared by text buttons, choices and the microphone.
- Native editors for both the next message and the answer to a specific question.
- Human-readable labels paired with measurements and their sources.

## Colors

**Primary.** Accent is deep teal by day and pale mint at night. It identifies Hermes as the speaker, actionable labels, microphone icons and added code. Profile submission pairs an accent fill with on-accent text. A prompt choice pairs accent text with the softer accent-surface fill.

**Semantic.** Danger marks field errors and removed code. Keep the error wording or the diff sign visible alongside the color.

**Neutral.** Paper fills the workspace. Surface lifts the question card and groups the lines of a fenced code excerpt. Ink carries the message, muted carries supporting labels, and line separates sections and outlines prompts. Night resources give every role a companion color. The primary token maps to Android’s accent resource. Other base names use hyphens for Android’s underscores. Night values come from the matching resources in `values-night`.

## Typography

Use the platform sans-serif for conversation and controls. Speaker names are small and bold (13 sp). Message text is larger and regular (17 sp). An in-thread question is an accessibility heading (18 sp, bold). The busy-state or connection message uses a quieter size (13–14 sp).

The thread title in the top bar can wrap to two lines at medium weight (20 sp). Full-screen panels give the page name (29 sp, bold) more prominence than their section headings (20 sp, bold). Workspace blocks use a heading (18 sp, bold) above their description (16 sp). Code and tool output use monospace. Usage values use monospace at 20 sp under a 16 sp label, including when the value reads “Indisponible”.

Frontmatter and browser previews use CSS pixels at the Android baseline density and default font scale. Use sp for text and dp for layout in the app. Android controls retain their native font metrics.

## Layout

The default discussion stacks a top bar, a flexible transcript and a composer. Its message and question gutters align at 18 dp. Give the editor the remaining width beside the microphone and the send or stop control. The editor grows to four visible lines. The activity reserves space for the system bars, display cutout and keyboard.

Suggested answers stack to the available width and grow with their labels. On the top bar, the title can ellipsize while the navigation and overflow controls keep their 48 dp square targets. Panel content scrolls within 20 dp side gutters, centering single-column sheets up to 680 dp and pairing two-section panels (settings, usage, reminder editor) up to 1040 dp with a 24 dp gap when both columns stay legible at the active font scale. Profile forms keep at least 22 dp at either side and center a column of up to 520 dp on wider windows; the Atelier centers up to 680 dp. [Panel framing](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/panel/PanelFrame.java) · [Adaptive geometry](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/AdaptiveLayout.java)

On phones, narrow windows and short landscape windows (below 720 dp wide or 400 dp tall, adjusted for font scale), the discussion list and active conversation occupy one pane so the transcript and composer stay in reach. On wider tablet windows, the discussion sidebar (232–360 dp) sits beside the active discussion with a 1 dp rule and a top-bar toggle (“Masquer les discussions” / “Afficher les discussions”). When a tool or restaurant guide opens and keeping the sidebar would leave less than 640 dp for the discussion pane, the sidebar auto-hides unless toggled open. Rotation, multi-window and split-screen geometry changes update layout in place without re-locking the profile or discarding drafts, cursor selections or open forms. [Conversation panes](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/thread/ConversationPanes.java)

Editable workspace sections use 16 dp side insets. Compact density reduces the vertical inset of a block from 12 dp to 5 dp. In a layout that wraps native content, each surrounding scroll region can take up to a third of the available height. The native region receives the remaining space.

A trial or pinned tool occupies the transcript area. Below 640 dp of available pane width, the tool takes that space and the return arrow restores the person’s place in the conversation. At 640 dp and above, the transcript and tool share the width equally, separated by a 1 dp rule. The composer stays below both. Keep the mounted form and editor when the window changes width. [Viewport implementation](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/thread/InstrumentViewport.java)

The 320 × 640 px phone previews show the reading column and stacked usage labels. The 1080 × 2340 px conversation captures show a long prompt scrolling above the composer and a dictated answer in its own field. These capture sizes illustrate the layouts. The app measures the available window in dp. [Discussion and usage journey](app/src/androidTest/java/org/llm4people/hermes/journey/DeviceJourney.java) · [Question and approval journey](app/src/androidTest/java/org/llm4people/hermes/journey/ConversationCardsJourney.java)

## Elevation & Depth

A thin outline and a change from paper to surface bring a question forward. Ordinary transcript turns sit directly on paper. Use the platform ripple on actionable surfaces and the platform window treatment for menus and dialogs.

## Shapes

Use 12 dp corners on the pending prompt and its suggested answers. A 1 dp line outlines the panel. Profile fields, the message composer and the question’s free-answer editor use an 8 dp radius with a 1 dp muted outline that becomes a 2 dp accent outline on focus. In each conversation editor, the outline encloses the text field and its microphone.

## Components

**Starting a conversation.** In the inbox, “Nouvelle conversation” and the saved thread titles share an 18 dp margin. When a discussion is confirmed empty and has no draft or pending request, “Quelques idées pour commencer” introduces three rows separated by fine rules. “Poser une question” focuses the composer. “Comprendre du code” and “Planifier mon travail” fill it with editable text. The suggestions sit just above the composer when they fit. When the available height is too short, the list scrolls from the heading. [Conversation starters](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/thread/ThreadStarters.java)

**Conversation.** “Vous” and “Hermes” name the speakers. Selectable messages can include code, diff signs and tool output. Longer messages offer a paged reader. Beside the draft, “Envoyer” has an outline while disabled and an accent fill when enabled. An active run that can be stopped shows a square beside “Arrêter”.

**Code excerpt.** Put fenced code on one light or dark surface across the full text column, preserving line breaks, indentation and selectable text. A settled turn previews up to four lines or 200 characters per block. A trailing code block shares its surface with “Ouvrir le code”. Use a supplied filename or language in the action label. Several blocks in the same display page use “Ouvrir un extrait…” to select one. The native reader pages through the complete source block and offers “Copier le contenu”. [Transcript formatter](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/thread/MessageText.java) · [Source reader](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/artifact/CodeReader.java)

**Question.** Put “Écouter” with its speaker icon below the question. A single-choice button submits immediately. Set “(choix recommandé)” in smaller type after its answer. Checkboxes allow multiple selections, and the person can write or dictate a custom answer. “Envoyer la réponse” gains an accent fill when an answer is ready. “Passer la question” is a muted text action at the trailing edge. Once answered, the question and reply read as ordinary turns in the restored conversation.

**Approval.** Lead with “Autoriser cette action ?” and describe the proposed effect. “Voir les détails” expands the exact command or edit when available. “Autoriser une fois” carries a check mark and “Refuser” a cross. Both use ink text and the same neutral outline. Give them equal width side by side when both labels and icons fit. Otherwise, stack them in full-width rows. While a response is being sent or checked, disable the submission controls and report progress in text. An unconfirmed response exposes “Vérifier la demande”.

**The Editable Draft Rule.** Dictation inserts or replaces text at the selection in the active editor. Sending takes a separate tap.

**Voice controls.** Pair the native text field with a 24 dp microphone inside a 48 dp icon button labelled “Dicter”. The square stop icon and an updated accessible label identify an active attempt. Announce recording, transcription and recovery messages through a polite live region. Keep the draft available for editing when recognition fails. Spoken playback uses its own “Écouter” and “Arrêter” actions.

**Navigation and workspace tools.** A labelled icon opens the discussion list or returns to the previous page. “Atelier” opens the workspace, where a full-width row combines a title and chevron into one accessible button. A proposed screen can contain native content, links, labelled values, sections and draft forms. Keep preview, apply and undo distinct. In an applied form, text fields and choice lists collect input for “Préparer un brouillon…”.

**Thread tool.** “Créer un outil pour ce fil” starts an editable request. The reply places the proposed tool’s own title and “Essayer dans la discussion” beneath the message. The selected proposal gains an accent-surface background. In the tool pane, put its return arrow, bold title and overflow in one header. A short teal status under the title identifies a trial or a pin. “Épingler à cette discussion” keeps the tool with that thread. When the tool is closed, its title and “Ouvrir” share a tinted row above the transcript.

**Form answers.** In the review tool, “Relecture de code” names the tool, “Vérifier la modification” names the form and “Fichier” labels the input. Use muted text for the form instruction and 15 sp medium labels for the fields. Mark optional fields “(facultatif)”. A missing required answer focuses the field and names it in the error message. The tonal button “Préparer un brouillon…” sits on the leading edge and opens “Relire le brouillon”. “Vers le brouillon” places the reviewed text in the discussion composer, where the person can still edit it before choosing “Envoyer”.

**Version comparison.** “Comparer les versions” opens on “Écarts”. Arrange “Épinglée”, “Écarts” and “Proposée” in one outlined native radio group. Inactive labels use muted ink. Use an ink fill for the selected pinned or differences view and an accent fill for the selected proposal. In either form view, the header shows that version’s title. While switching between versions, keep each form’s unsent answers with its version.

Group differences by section. An added or removed field leads with its name in bold, followed by a muted line such as “Champ ajouté · Choix obligatoire”. Declared options appear as bullets. For a changed value, align the earlier and proposed values beside “Avant” and “Après”. Use muted ink for the earlier value and ink for the proposal. Below the comparison, “Adopter cette version” replaces the pin. “Garder la version épinglée” returns to the saved version. After adoption, “Annuler la modification” restores the previous state. [Native comparison](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/artifact/InstrumentPreview.java) · [Thread tool journey](app/src/androidTest/java/org/llm4people/hermes/journey/ThreadInstrumentJourney.java)

**Discussion settings.** Under “Modèle et fournisseur”, one tinted row combines the current provider and model or “Suivre le profil” with the action “Choisir le modèle”. The searchable provider and model lists mark the saved choice with a radio indicator. Under “Ensembles d’outils”, inherited sets show their checkmarks with muted names. Where the server supports selection, clearing “Utiliser les outils du profil” enables the set checkboxes. Indent the individual tool chooser or member names under their set. [Discussion settings](app/src/main/java/org/llm4people/hermes/panel/SessionSettings.java)

**The Measurement Label Rule.** Place the French measure name directly above the value. Give the value one accessible description containing both the name and value.

**Usage.** Group cumulative counters under “Cumul de la discussion”. Group device timings under “Dernière réponse mesurée sur cet appareil”. Distinguish known billed and estimated costs, show pricing provenance when available, and display “Indisponible” for a missing measurement.

## Do's and Don'ts

### Do

- Keep a pending decision in its own conversation, with its choices and answer field together.
- Preserve French action labels when text wraps or the input method is visible.
- Keep code and tool output selectable for inspection.

### Don't

- Don't send a draft when a person dictates or taps a starter.
- Don't turn an unavailable measurement into a zero.
- Don't treat a recommended answer as permission to act.
